1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,113
Ask an AI to write SQL and you get a query that looks right.

2
00:00:04,133 --> 00:00:08,660
The question I care about is: how would you know when it is wrong?

3
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:23,789
My final project will be CheckedSQL: a command-line tool that turns English questions into SQL for a public sample database, runs every query, and checks the result against an answer I verified by hand.

4
00:00:23,809 --> 00:00:30,230
It will be a GitHub repository with a test set, saved results, and a failure analysis.

5
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:34,401
I looked at asking a chat assistant directly, and Vanna.ai.

6
00:00:34,421 --> 00:00:42,035
They generate SQL, but they do not give me a small, reproducible record of which answers were wrong and why.

7
00:00:42,055 --> 00:00:43,166
CheckedSQL will.

8
00:00:43,186 --> 00:00:48,750
I am not claiming it is more accurate — only that its mistakes will be visible.

9
00:00:48,770 --> 00:00:52,674
The reader is a hiring manager screening an SDE candidate.

10
00:00:52,694 --> 00:01:02,280
In the first minute they will open the README, see how many questions matched and why the others failed, and rerun the check with one command.

11
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:18,421
It will show three things from this course: a prompt contract — the model must output exactly one SELECT or a refusal; verification — every query runs on a read-only database; and evaluation — a fixed test set with saved results.

12
00:01:18,441 --> 00:01:22,720
The evidence will be the result files and the commit history.

13
00:01:22,740 --> 00:01:24,547
Here is what exists today.

14
00:01:24,567 --> 00:01:32,066
A test set of twenty questions and a draft eval script, written with Claude's help on October twenty-first.

15
00:01:32,086 --> 00:01:37,688
That same day I ran every answer query on the real database, and all twenty ran.

16
00:01:37,708 --> 00:01:40,920
The part that calls the model has not run yet.

17
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:44,800
In scope: read-only questions on one sample database.

18
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:48,094
Out: no web interface and no other databases.

19
00:01:48,114 --> 00:01:58,050
The first piece I will make run is one question end to end; you will know it ran because a results file will show the SQL and a verdict.

20
00:01:58,070 --> 00:02:01,459
What could sink it: the checker itself can be wrong.

21
00:02:01,479 --> 00:02:03,098
It already happened once.

22
00:02:03,118 --> 00:02:11,621
When I checked my own answers by hand, one of them turned out to be arbitrary: four years were tied, and my query just picked one.

23
00:02:11,641 --> 00:02:18,570
So every gold answer gets checked by hand, and checker mistakes are counted as their own failure category.

24
00:02:18,590 --> 00:02:19,729
Why this project?

25
00:02:19,749 --> 00:02:23,137
It's a mix, and honestly it leans toward learning.

26
00:02:23,157 --> 00:02:33,634
I'm a software engineering master's student applying for SDE roles, and I wanted something a stranger can check, not something that just looks impressive.

27
00:02:33,654 --> 00:02:42,767
I taught myself Python and SQL basics from YouTube, but I don't have a repo that proves it yet, so this project will be that evidence.

28
00:02:42,787 --> 00:02:49,788
What's new to me is calling Claude from my own code, building a test set, and measuring where it fails.

29
00:02:49,808 --> 00:02:54,150
I chose something small on purpose, so I can finish it honestly.

30
00:02:54,170 --> 00:03:00,101
CheckedSQL: not an AI that is always right, but one whose mistakes you can see.

31
00:03:00,121 --> 00:03:01,080
I'm Yuhao Fu.
